WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.285
The most important development work often happens before any screens are built.

00:00:04.285 --> 00:00:07.773
Start by defining the audience, their current level of knowledge,

00:00:07.773 --> 00:00:11.261
and what they are expected to do after the course.

00:00:11.261 --> 00:00:15.447
A useful course goal is not simply that learners understand a topic.

00:00:15.447 --> 00:00:17.888
It should describe a decision, action, behavior,

00:00:17.888 --> 00:00:20.679
or judgment they need to perform more reliably.

00:00:20.679 --> 00:00:24.864
Separate what learners need to know from what learners need to do.

00:00:24.864 --> 00:00:28.352
Background information may be necessary, but it should support performance.

00:00:28.352 --> 00:00:32.537
For example, a learner may need to recognize when a process applies,

00:00:32.537 --> 00:00:35.328
choose the correct step, avoid a common mistake,

00:00:35.328 --> 00:00:37.769
or explain a requirement to a customer.

00:00:37.769 --> 00:00:41.955
Those outcomes lead to better screen design and better assessment questions than

00:00:41.955 --> 00:00:43.699
a long list of facts.

00:00:43.699 --> 00:00:45.791
Planning also defines scope and constraints.

00:00:45.791 --> 00:00:48.930
Decide the course length, structure, interaction level, delivery format,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.372
accessibility expectations, completion rules, and assessment style.

00:00:51.372 --> 00:00:53.813
Some courses need a polished narrated experience.

00:00:53.813 --> 00:00:55.906
Others need a concise reference-like structure.

00:00:55.906 --> 00:00:58.348
Some must report completion to an LMS.

00:00:58.348 --> 00:01:01.138
Others may be used as informal enablement material.

00:01:01.138 --> 00:01:02.882
Storyboarding and scripting reduce rework.

00:01:02.882 --> 00:01:07.765
A storyboard does not need to be elaborate, but it should show module flow,

00:01:07.765 --> 00:01:10.904
key screens, narration, media ideas, interactions, and quiz alignment.

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:16.136
Opening the authoring tool before the instructional plan is clear usually leads to rearranging screens,

00:01:16.136 --> 00:01:18.229
rewriting narration, and rebuilding interactions later.

00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:21.717
Planning is how a course avoids becoming a decorated document.

00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:25.902
A strong planning document should be short enough for stakeholders to read

00:01:25.902 --> 00:01:27.995
but specific enough to guide development.

00:01:27.995 --> 00:01:31.483
Include the audience, goal, module outline, assessment approach, accessibility expectations,

00:01:31.483 --> 00:01:33.227
review roles, and publishing target.

00:01:33.227 --> 00:01:37.412
That document becomes the reference point when someone asks to add unrelated

00:01:37.412 --> 00:01:40.900
content or change the course direction late in the project.
